ust  fulfill your promise and don't  let the people die volunteer jeff swartz  helped ahmad and other afghan families  settle in the united states  he says the u.s has a moral obligation  to protect those  who helped its troops this is a country  that has landed a rover on mars  and we're sitting here telling that  there's a process that has to be  followed  and we can't get 18 000 human beings  that whose lives are at risk  ahmad is lucky he applied for his visa  early  at the current rate his brother might  get his approved within three years  long after the u.s army has left  afghanistan  and for more i'm joined tonight by adam  weinstein he's a research fellow at the  quincy institute for responsible  statecraft he's an  expert on afghanistan where he also  served as a u.s  marine he joins me tonight from  washington d.c  and imran faraz he is an austrian afghan  journalist and  author he joins me tonight from  stuttgart here in germany to both of you  gentlemen it's good to have you  on the program imran let me start with  you is there a future for people  like those interpreters in afghanistan  first of all thanks for having me um i  think it's really difficult to say if  they have a positive future there  i mean it's not just about interpreters  who are still stuck in afghanistan  but also many other people you know the  nato personnel  that was built and recruited during the  last 20 years  all over the country is rather big and  many of them  are stuck because of you know  bureaucratic  uh issues that are not really reasonable  if you know the realities on the ground  i have just been busy with a case of an  afghan who used to work with the german  bundeswehr  he was not an interpreter but he was  responsible for the whole  telecommunication  of the troops and he regularly worked  with the troops every day for years  but now they are not interested in him  because he did not have  direct contract with the troops he  worked for a private firm and the  private firm  was working for the troops but you know  on the ground  for the taliban and other militants to  hunt down such people  this doesn't make a real difference so  does he is he he has to stay in  afghanistan then  yes exactly the german foreign ministry  literally said that they are not  interested in his case because  he did not have a direct contract so he  should take  care of him himself privately it's not  their business  okay adam let me come to you the united  states has pledged to evacuate these  translators  interpreters with their families and  that is taking place at the same time  it looks it looks a little bit like it's  an afterthought  is it uh well thanks for having me um  and uh i i'd have to say it is a bit of  an afterthought because  uh there was a lot of back and forth  over the withdrawal decision itself  and the cost of that indecisiveness  across both the trump and biden  administrations frankly  um and especially in the last few months  what was that there wasn't enough  planning  about the these contingencies like the  sib applicants and it might even be the  case that the  the pentacon uh was was dragging its  feet a bit  about a decision a withdrawal decision  that it didn't want to make  um that being said uh all  bureaucratic processes are tedious and  complex and i think what's necessary  here is for the biden administration  to cut through the red tape not allow a  bureaucracy to be used as an  excuse for allowing those who directly  supported us to be killed  and and simply get these people out it's  a task that is within the control of the  united states  most conditions in afghanistan are not  in the control of the united states  but getting the sib applicants out those  people who helped us directly  is something that is very much in our  control and it should be done  uh with uh speed and dispatch yeah and  we know that that is happening now they  they're being brought  to virginia while their visa  applications are being  processed and ron um for afghans  does your opinion of the u.s withdrawal  does it depend on  who you are and where you are  yes in my experience it does i think  what we often hear  in many western media reports is that  people are concerned  and of course many people are concerned  about what is happening  but what i experienced on the ground  is that it it's you know it's often very  different  and opinions differ from each other it's  often very heterogenic  also you have to consider that the life  of someone who lives in kabul in the  capital  is very different than the life of  someone who lives in rural afghanistan  like you know  in northern babylon province or in  northeastern  cornell province and you hear a lot of  different opinions about the withdrawal  and i think that uh  yeah that's that's what's going on over  there well um what do you hear dude do  people think that  a return of the taliban is in return of  the taliban to power do they think  that's now  inevitable  you know in kabul for example you hear  people who of course  fear such a return they think that a  scenario like in 1992  when the communist regime was toppled by  the mujahideen  would take place again and then you have  civil war and everything  in my opinion you already have civil war  in afghanistan if you consider what is  going on all over the country  but you know there are other regions  that have suffered from the american  occupation for example from airstrikes  drone strikes night raids  and if you talk to these people they're  rather happy that the americans leave  that's what was my experience for  example during my last reporting trip in  some of  the areas that i visited and  i think that a lot of afghans also know  that uh  you know foreign occupiers foreign  military forces  are not a constant solution to  solve the conflict uh in their country  yeah well they've had plenty of  experience with foreign occupiers that  is for sure  adam how do you see this is it is it  inevitable that the taliban returned to  power  or is that a message that the taliban  is trying to push  well i think it's a bit of both and look  you introduced me as an expert on  afghanistan and that's very flattering  i don't really consider myself one and i  think there's very few  uh genuine afghanistan experts in the  world and the reason i say that is  predictions about the political  trajectory of afghanistan  from anyone should always come with a  grain of salt  because they often times are wrong i  think that the taliban are  in an ascendant posture right now and  they've certainly taken a lot of  districts  we do have to remember that the and dsf  often retakes strategic districts  the taliban don't have enough manpower  to hold all of these districts  permanently  many of these outposts that the and dsf  had shouldn't really have existed  to begin with they were simply points on  a map that could  you know be pointed to and create the  illusion of government control over an  area  but in reality that outpost was on a  road that was controlled by the taliban  and couldn't be resupplied  and the reason the afghan soldiers  surrendered might have been to  lack of supplies or lack of ammunition  and and so i think the a and dsf  or the afghan security forces are going  to have to  reimagine the way they fight without  us air support and without the  logistical support  of the u.s military machine and they're  going to have to become more mobile and  coalesce around  population centers and of course these  are recommendations that u.s advisors  have  told the ghani administration uh for  some time now and told the afghan  government in general for it for years  um but there was a lack of political  will uh to  institute these reforms because uh there  it was bad optics or it might look like  uh the afghan government was giving up  territory  uh but now um there's not time  to consider optics the afghan government  has to focus um  on on uh keeping territory and regaining  strategic territory  and ultimately the only real solution  uh to what could be described as a civil  war with very powerful  third-party intermediaries uh or  intervenors  such as pakistan in the united states is  a political settlement  between afghans and at some point the  government and the taliban are going to  have to  meet each other halfway or else uh what  i think we're going to see is um  an ever intensifying uh civil war but i  do not think a taliban dominated  afghanistan  is inevitable at this moment okay i mean  well that's the future let's talk a  little bit  about the past adam you you served as a  marine in afghanistan and  you've said that you thought your task  was nation building  i want you to take a listen to what u.s  president biden what he said recently  about the mission in afghanistan  we did not go to afghanistan to  nation-build  and it's the right and the  responsibility of afghan people alone  to decide their future and how they want  to run their country  so adam the question is did the uf stay  too long  in afghanistan or should it never have  been there in the first place  well i think the united states was in a  position where it had to respond to 911  but that response could have been far  more limited and focused on  counterterrorism  than what it uh became and president  biden is correct when he says that the  original intent  and the original authorization uh by  congress for that matter  uh was not to engage in nation-building  activities  but there was a mission creep and uh  the mission creep and became  larger and larger to justify a longer  u.s presence to the american people and  and to the world  imran do you agree mission creep  yeah i think adam hinted towards some  really important points  regarding the political will of the  afghan national forces who are fighting  on the ground  i think that it's not just about  political will it's about the fact that  the afghan military is a deeply corrupt  kind of  institution that has a lot of problems i  mean  what adam described partly uh  you know you could you could go a bit  deeper and describe for example  the so-called ghost soldiers that  existed all over the country  you know soldiers who just existed on  the paper while  high-ranking military officials  literally stole the salaries of these  people  to make themselves rich and i visited in  march i visited  uh you know a couple of afghan soldiers  fighting  against the taliban in cornell province  and if you see these people you see what  the problem is they don't they literally  don't have  anything and are just on themselves  and uh i think regarding the nation  building issue  uh it's not just about what joe biden  said recently  uh from the very beginning the whole  intervention  was a failure because this the amount  they met the americans and their allies  started to work with very questionable  people on the ground in afghanistan  uh you know warlords drag barons  dracquarones and deeply corrupt  politicians  who all of them you know served in the  government of hamid karzai  and others are still still having high  ranking positions  and with such people it was very very  difficult from the very beginning  to get proper results well i've got  about a minute  left so let me ask boys of you gentlemen  i'll start with you imran and then i'll  hear from you adam what's going to be  the legacy of  the us presence in afghanistan imran you  first  i think the legacy of the us presence is  that  not the single goal of the us  intervention  was actually reached uh high-ranking  terrorists you know militant leaders  like osama bin laden  muhammad of the taliban all of them  were not hit by the so-called precise  operations of the americans  some of them even died naturally instead  many other people were killed  and dozens of people you know  thousands of thousands of people uh on  the ground  were radicalized joined the taliban  instead of  you know solving the terror problem in  afghanistan  we just see that much more terrorism has  been created  adam what about you the legacy is going  to be that the united states  should think twice about engaging in  interventions abroad because  afghanistan has demonstrated that the  the us military cannot  uh shape conditions on the ground but  perhaps the more important legacy is  what is the legacy going to be for  afghans uh and and ultimately are the  taliban and the afghan government going  to be able to  meet each other halfway and come up with  a political settlement  and the united states might be able to  revise its its legacy if it remains  engaged economically and diplomatically  even after u.s boots  leave the ground in afghanistan in fact  this is going to be a real test  for whether the united states actually  values diplomacy  uh when it is decoupled from military  intervention abroad  adam weinstein a research fellow at the  quincy institute for responsible  statecraft  and a former u.s marine and imran ferraz  an austrian afghan journalist  and author to both of you gentlemen we  appreciate your time and your insights  tonight thank you  thank you  you

